To blend multiple audio files using Java, you can use the popular open-source library called SoX (Simple Operations on Audio). Here's how you can use it:

1. First, download the necessary SoX libraries for your operating system from their website (https://sox.sourceforge.net/download.html) and include them in your project's classpath.

2. Next, create a new Java class named `AudioBlender` with the following code:

```java
import org.sox4j.*;
import java.io.File;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class AudioBlender {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        List<String> inputFiles = getInputFiles(); // gets list of input audio files
        String outputFile = "output.wav"; // name of output audio file

        SoxRewriter rewriter = new SoxRewriter()
            .input("in")
            .inputs(inputFiles)
            .outputFormat(SoxFormat.WAV)
            .output(outputFile);

        // add volume adjustment
        rewriter.addEffect(new VolumeEffect(-3));
        // add fade in/out effect
        rewriter.addEffect(new FadeEffect(500));

        // start blending process
        rewriter.run();

        System.out.println("Audio blended successfully!");
    }

    private static List<String> getInputFiles() {
        List<String> inputFiles = new ArrayList<>();
        inputFiles.add("birds.wav");
        inputFiles.add("leaves.wav");
        inputFiles.add("cicadas.wav");
        inputFiles.add("children_playing.wav");
        return inputFiles;
    }
}
```

