To add a Gaussian filter before applying the Sobel operator, you can create a Gaussian kernel with a standard deviation of your choice (e.g., `sigma = 3`), convolve it with the grayscale image using the `conv2` function, and then apply the Sobel operator on the filtered image. Here's the modified code:

```matlab
% Load input image and convert to grayscale
input_image = imread('image.jpg');
gray_image = rgb2gray(input_image);

% Set standard deviation for Gaussian filter
sigma = 3;

% Create Gaussian kernel
[gaussian_kernel, gaussian_kernel_size] = fspecial('gaussian', [3 3], sigma);

% Filter the input image with Gaussian kernel
filtered_image = imfilter(gray_image, gaussian_kernel, 'replicates');

% Define Sobel operators in x and y directions
sobel_x = [-1 0 1; -2 0 2; -1 0 1];
sobel_y = [-1 -2 -1; 0 0 0; 1 2 1];

% Apply Sobel operator in x direction
edge_image_x = conv2(double(filtered_image), sobel_x, 'same');

% Apply Sobel operator in y direction
edge_image_y = conv2(double(filtered_image), sobel_y, 'same');

% Combine edges in both directions and display result
edge_image = sqrt(edge_image_x.^2 + edge_image_y.^2);
imshow(edge_image);
```

In this example, we first load the input image, convert it to grayscale, and set the standard deviation for the Gaussian filter. We then create a Gaussian kernel using the `fspecial` function, filter the grayscale image with the Gaussian kernel using the `imfilter` function, and apply the Sobel operator on the filtered image as before. Note that we also converted the filtered image to double precision to avoid integer overflow during the computation of gradients.

